vcxsrv-fakemonitor -- make the X server report one monitor
==========================================================

Stock VcXsrv 1.14.2.1 answers RandR with nothing at all:

    $ xrandr
    Screen 0: minimum 0 x 0, current 1600 x 900, maximum 4096 x 4096

No output, no CRTC, no mode, no monitor. Clients that look for a display before
opening a window have nothing to find.

After patching:

    $ xrandr
    Screen 0: minimum 1600 x 900, current 1600 x 900, maximum 1600 x 900
    default connected 1600x900+0+0 0mm x 0mm
       1600x900      60.00*

Why this is a patch and not a proxy or a library shim
-----------------------------------------------------

Outputs are created by the server, in C, at screen init. The protocol has no
request that makes one, so nothing outside the process can add a monitor --
not a client, not an X proxy, not a faked libXrandr. A proxy that rewrites
RandR replies has to reimplement the extension and only fools whoever is
pointed at it; a faked libXrandr misses everything speaking xcb-randr directly,
which includes Chromium/CEF. Patching the server fixes it for every client at
once, which is the only place the fix is universal.

And it is a small patch, because the server nearly does this already. See
fakemonitor.S: xorg-server contains a routine that manufactures exactly one
CRTC and one connected output for drivers that only speak RandR 1.0, and XWin
never reaches it because its rrGetInfo hook forgets to register a screen size.
The patch replaces that hook with one that registers it. Everything after --
creating the output, marking it connected, building the mode, lighting the
CRTC, answering every query -- is the server's own untouched code.

Applying it
-----------

    ./patch-vcxsrv.py vcxsrv.exe vcxsrv-patched.exe

Needs gcc, ld and objcopy (any host binutils; the output is raw machine code,
not an ELF). It verifies six byte signatures in the input first and refuses to
write anything if the binary is not the 1.14.2.1 build the addresses were read
out of, so it cannot quietly corrupt a different server.

The edit is two things: a `.moco` section holding the 265-byte replacement
hook, and one displacement inside winRandRInit() repointed at it. The image is
byte-identical everywhere else.

Installing it on a box
----------------------

Back up the original, stop the server, swap, restart:

    copy /y %D%\vcxsrv.exe %D%\vcxsrv.exe.orig
    taskkill /f /im vcxsrv.exe
    copy /y vcxsrv-patched.exe %D%\vcxsrv.exe
    call xstart1142.bat C:\PROGRA~1\MoCoLinux

where %D% is ...\MoCoLinux\vcxsrv1142. Restarting the X server kills the
guest's existing X clients but nothing else: the wire opens a fresh connection
to 127.0.0.1:6000 per client, so new ones simply reconnect and neither the
guest nor any daemon needs restarting.

Reinstalling VcXsrv reverts this. Reapply it after any reinstall.

Known cosmetic wart
-------------------

    xrandr: Failed to get size of gamma for output default

The CRTC has no gamma ramp, because the routine that creates it never sets one.
That is stock upstream behaviour for this code path, not something the patch
introduced, and xrandr carries on regardless.

`xrandr --listmonitors` still fails with "RandR 1.5 not supported". That is the
server's version, unrelated to outputs -- 1.14 implements RandR 1.4, and the
monitor list is a 1.5 request. Plain `xrandr` and every client that enumerates
outputs works.
